# scripts/pins.env - the SINGLE source of truth for every third-party artifact
# the release pipeline downloads.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (DRAGON-531): the pins used to live in each fetcher, and
# they drifted immediately. The Linux container built ffmpeg 8.0 while the macOS
# package bundled 8.1.2, so the same release shipped two different encoders on
# two platforms, and nothing but a human reading both files caught it. Every
# fetcher now reads THIS file, which makes "the same versions everywhere" true by
# construction instead of by vigilance.
#
# Read by:
#   scripts/fetch-mac-vendor.sh     (sources it directly)
#   scripts/fetch-win-vendor.ps1    (parses it into a hashtable)
#   .github/workflows/release.yml   (build-linux, the container ffmpeg build)
#   justfile                        (the linux `dist` recipe, same build)
#   scripts/appimage/Dockerfile     (the AppImage base image: ffmpeg, x264,
#                                    tesseract, the AppImage runtime)
#
# FORMAT: plain KEY=value, one per line, no spaces around `=`, no shell
# expansion. That is deliberate. bash can `source` it as-is, PowerShell parses it
# in three lines, and the container step needs no parser at all. TOML would buy
# nothing and cost a parser in bash.
#
# A value containing a SPACE must be double-quoted. Several are MIRROR LISTS,
# one space-separated URL per mirror, tried in order until one succeeds:
# `LIBPNG_URL`, `FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL`, `FFMPEG_SOURCE_ASC_URL`, `FFMPEG_GPG_KEY_URL`.
# bash `source` reads an unquoted `KEY=a b` as "set KEY=a, then run the command
# b", which is exactly the bug that broke the mac fetch once; the PowerShell
# parser strips the quotes to match.
#
# A mirror list is availability, never trust: every one of them is still gated by
# the same sha256 (and for ffmpeg, the same pinned GPG fingerprint), so a hostile
# mirror fails exactly as a hostile upstream would.
#
# CHANGING A PIN: update the version, the URL and the hash TOGETHER, and never
# add a hash you have not downloaded and computed yourself. Every fetcher treats
# a mismatch as fatal and never retries: the only two causes are an upstream file
# changing under a URL that promised to be immutable, or a pin edited without its
# hash, and neither gets better on a second download.
#
# THERE ARE TWO CLASSES OF PIN HERE, and how much a move costs depends entirely
# on which one you are moving (DRAGON-675):
#
#   A PREBUILT third-party BINARY. Every move is a QUALIFICATION EVENT, however
#   small the version step looks, because we did not choose what it was compiled
#   against and the compile-time choices are not all readable from the file. The
#   binary's demands on the USER'S MACHINE ride along with it, silently.
#
#   SOURCE we build ourselves. A version move really is just a refresh, because
#   every floor the result imposes is one of OUR pins in this file, and moving
#   one of those is a visible edit with a ticket attached.
#
# That distinction is not theory. This file used to reason, about the Windows
# ffmpeg, that "a patch bump is a pin refresh rather than a qualification event"
# because 9.0 and 9.0.1 ship the same sonames. The sonames were indeed the same.
# What changed was that 9.0.1 had been built against nv-codec-headers 13.1 where
# 9.0 used 13.0, which moved the minimum NVIDIA driver from ~570 to 610.00, so
# every Windows user below that lost NVENC, silently fell back to libx265, and
# their recordings died about fourteen seconds in (DRAGON-671). Nothing in the
# shipped DLL reports the number: both halves of the error are formatted at
# runtime from constants compiled in from the headers, and `-version` /
# `-buildconf` do not carry it. So the reasoning was not merely wrong, it was
# unfalsifiable from the artifact, which is what makes the class distinction
# worth writing down rather than the individual mistake.
#
# The answer for the artifact that caused it was to stop inheriting: the Windows
# ffmpeg is now OUR cross-build (scripts/ffmpeg-win/, DRAGON-675) against the
# NVCODEC_URL pin the Linux one already used. Where inheriting is unavoidable,
# the floor must be ASSERTED at fetch time instead: `fetch-mac-vendor.sh` reads
# the macOS build's LC_BUILD_VERSION and fails against FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_MINOS,
# which is possible there only because a Mach-O carries its deployment target
# where a PE carries nothing about NVENC.
#
# NOTE ON REDUNDANCY: the version appears both as its own key and inside the
# URLs. That is on purpose, because the consumers use the version for the
# extracted directory name, so a URL and a version that disagree fail loudly at
# the `cd` rather than silently building the wrong thing.

# ── ffmpeg ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 9.0 (DRAGON-631), moved from 8.1.2 WITH the test pass the old pin's comment
# demanded: the full real-ffmpeg suite (`record::media_clock_e2e_tests`, all 15,
# plus `record::av_sync_tests`) ran green against ffmpeg 9.0 before this pin
# moved, and `ffmpeg-next` moved to 9.0.0 in the same change so the zero-copy
# bindings and the bundled libraries stay ONE generation. The ffmpeg-8 era
# defenses (MuxerWatchdog for the first-write wedge, the live `-itsoffset` ban
# for the ~200ms scheduler stall) were measured on 8 and STAY: they are bounded
# defenses, cheap when the bug is absent, and nobody has proven 9 free of them.
#
# ── ALL THREE PLATFORMS SHIP ONE FFMPEG VERSION. THE MAC IS THE FLOOR. ───────
#
# Owner decision, 2026-08-13. Linux and Windows build from the source tarball
# below, so either could move on its own the day a patch lands. macOS cannot:
# its ffmpeg is a PREBUILT from martin-riedl.de, and we get the version they
# choose to publish. So the rule is that this pin does not move until the mac
# build exists too, even when the other two are ready. A patch of drift between
# platforms is not worth having three recording pipelines to reason about.
#
# WORKED EXAMPLE, which is why this is written here rather than remembered:
# 9.0.1 was released and IS available as source, GPG-verified (sha256
# cf38e0e28c7e5605942c4a77755349b0145804a397af37eb1fb4c77cb237f635). It was not
# taken, because martin-riedl.de had published no 9.0.1 macOS arm64 build: their
# `latest/release` redirect still resolved to build 1785863997_9.0 and their
# listing did not mention 9.0.1 at all. Re-check that before proposing the move
# again.
#
# AND THE THING THAT MAKES THIS CONFUSING: 9.0.1 is NOT barred for being 9.0.1.
# The NVIDIA driver floor that caused DRAGON-671 comes from NVCODEC_VERSION, an
# INDEPENDENT pin further down, not from the ffmpeg version. gyan's PREBUILT
# 9.0.1 happened to be compiled against nv-codec-headers 13.1; the source is
# innocent. Now that we build the Windows one ourselves against our own header
# pin, 9.0.1 would carry the same ~570 floor 9.0 does. Whoever moves this pin
# next should not go looking for a reason to fear the version number.
FFMPEG_VERSION=9.0

# Linux release artifact: the SOURCE tarball, built inside the Rocky 9 container
# only to supply the headers the zero-copy encoder compiles against.
#
# This is the ONLY artifact any upstream here signs, and it is verified by that
# GPG SIGNATURE rather than by checksum alone. The distinction is real: a hash we
# carry only proves the bytes still match what we saw when the pin was made,
# while the signature proves the FFmpeg project produced them. The fingerprint
# below is published on ffmpeg.org/download.html, so it is independently
# confirmable rather than trust-on-first-use, and the build requires the
# signature to be made by exactly it, so a compromised key URL supplying both a
# key and a matching signature still fails. The sha256 is a cheap first gate.
#
# MIRRORED, space-separated, tried in order, first success wins. Same shape as
# LIBPNG_URL below and consumed the same way.
#
# WHY (2026-08-06): ffmpeg.org took the v0.29.0 release build down. It was not
# even a clean outage, which would at least fail fast: DNS resolved, TCP 443
# opened, and TLS then reset the connection (`curl` exit 35), intermittently, so
# a plain retry could just as easily fail again. ffmpeg.org is the ONLY host
# these three files came from, and ALL FOUR Linux jobs build ffmpeg from source,
# so one flapping upstream took out every Linux artifact at once.
#
# The second URL is our own copy on the PUBLIC repo, tag `vendor-mirror`, a
# PRERELEASE so it can never become `releases/latest` and displace the manifest
# the in-app updater reads. GitHub release assets are immutable uploads, unlike a
# generated `/archive/` tarball whose bytes can change under a pinned hash (the
# reason X264_URL below takes Debian's pool rather than a GitLab archive).
#
# Mirroring adds AVAILABILITY, never trust: the sha256 gates the tarball and the
# signature must be by the pinned fingerprint, so a hostile mirror fails exactly
# as a hostile upstream would. Refresh the mirror whenever FFMPEG_VERSION moves,
# or the fallback silently stops matching and only helps until the primary breaks.
FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz"
FFMPEG_SOURCE_ASC_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz.asc https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz.asc"
FFMPEG_SOURCE_SHA256=7f607a00dd0d28a729d5a4811205812eef01cf6ef6155025febb6f36a9062d52
FFMPEG_GPG_KEY_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-devel.asc https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-devel.asc"
FFMPEG_GPG_FPR=FCF986EA15E6E293A5644F10B4322F04D67658D8

# macOS package: static arm64 binaries from martin-riedl.de, which keeps every
# release build at a permanent per-build URL and publishes a `.sha256` beside
# each artifact (the hashes below match those published files). No signature is
# offered, so a pinned hash is the honest best available here.
#
# STILL A PREBUILT, deliberately, and DRAGON-675 did not change that. Read the
# two-class rule at the top of this file: a prebuilt's compile-time choices ride
# along invisibly, which is what cost us the Windows NVENC floor. macOS gets the
# cheap guard instead of the expensive fix, and the reason is that the bug class
# is different there in both directions.
#
#   NVENC CANNOT EXIST on Apple Silicon. There is no NVIDIA hardware, the mac
#   hardware tier is VideoToolbox, and nv-codec-headers are not compiled in at
#   all. So the specific failure DRAGON-671 chased is structurally impossible
#   here, and building our own mac ffmpeg (which needs a Mac or osxcross) would
#   be large work for a problem that cannot occur.
#
#   The floor macOS DOES inherit is this build's MINIMUM OS VERSION, and unlike
#   the NVENC one it is READABLE FROM THE FILE: every Mach-O carries
#   LC_BUILD_VERSION, so `vtool -show-build` (or `otool -l`) reports it. That is
#   the whole reason mac gets an assertion where Windows needed a build.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed: this build reports minos 12.0 (SDK 12.3), which is
# BELOW the .app's own LSMinimumSystemVersion of 13.0, so the sidecar is not
# what sets the product's floor today. `fetch-mac-vendor.sh` fails the fetch on
# any change, in either direction. A RISE above 13.0 would mean users on a
# supported macOS install the app and find no ffmpeg (a dyld failure at launch,
# loud, unlike the silent NVENC downgrade); a DROP is harmless but still means
# the upstream build changed under us and somebody should know.
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_BASE_URL=https://ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de/download/macos/arm64
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_BUILD=1785863997_9.0
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_SHA256=5267ef149ee0d208057a1b316aac079b661b0476574dee5da7d225769773c603
FFPROBE_MACOS_ARM64_SHA256=7778fbb533fb60d3336cbd9a9e51eced71658f020b570c7203590c1c41d42f50
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_MINOS=12.0

# Windows package: OUR OWN cross-build, mirrored on our own release.
#
# WHY OWN IT, the short version: what an ffmpeg binary demands of the user's
# machine is decided when it is COMPILED, and for a prebuilt we neither choose
# those inputs nor can read them back out afterwards. See the two-class rule at
# the top of this file. `scripts/ffmpeg-win/` cross-compiles it with mingw-w64,
# in a container, from the same GPG-verified FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL tarball the Linux
# artifacts compile and against the same NVCODEC_URL headers, and it emits a
# `.7z` laid out exactly like the gyan.dev archive it replaced, so
# `fetch-win-vendor.ps1` consumes it unchanged.
#
# ── NOTHING BUILDS FFMPEG AS PART OF A NORMAL BUILD ──────────────────────────
#
# Not GitHub CI, not `just dev`, not the MSI. The container build is a PRODUCER
# that runs ONCE when a pin moves; its output is PUBLISHED; everything else
# FETCHES it and is gated by the SHA-256 below. `just dev` therefore downloads
# the exact bytes a customer's MSI carries, which is the property that makes a
# dev-box recording a real test of the shipped encoder. CI needs no container,
# no warm image and no build cache: a hosted Windows runner cannot run a Linux
# container anyway, and `actions/cache` is the wrong home for a release-critical
# artifact (7-day eviction, size caps).
#
# MOVING A PIN IS THEREFORE THREE STEPS, IN ONE COMMIT:
#   1. `just ffmpeg-win`
#   2. gh release upload vendor-mirror target/artifacts/<the .7z> \
#          --repo Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit
#   3. paste the URL and SHA-256 the build printed into the two keys below
#
# THE `vendor-mirror` TAG, not a new one: it already exists, it already holds
# exactly this class of artifact (the ffmpeg source tarballs, and the gyan
# archive this replaces), and it is a PRERELEASE, so it can never become
# `releases/latest` and displace the manifest the in-app updater reads. A second
# tag would double the places to look for one answer.
#
# THE ASSET NAME ENCODES WHAT IT WAS BUILT FROM:
#   ffmpeg-<version>-nvcodec-<nv-codec-headers tag>-cck<our revision>-win64-shared
# The middle field is there because it IS the NVIDIA driver floor, which is the
# whole reason this build exists. Reading the URL below tells you which floor
# you are about to ship without downloading anything.
#
# THE SHA-256 IS RECORDED, NOT PREDICTED. This build is NOT byte-reproducible: a
# PE carries a link timestamp and the 7z container stores mtimes, so two runs
# over identical sources differ. That is honest and it is fine, because the hash
# pins the artifact we ACTUALLY published rather than proving a determinism we
# do not have; the INPUTS are what is pinned, checksummed and (for ffmpeg
# itself) signed. Never carry a hash over from a previous build.
#
# ── The history, kept because every step of it was reasonable ────────────────
#
# WHY gyan.dev, after two years on BtbN: BtbN builds master plus the 8.1 and
# 7.1 release branches and, as of 2026-08-10, no 9.0 branch at all, so it had
# no 9.0 to pin. gyan.dev is the other builder ffmpeg.org itself links to,
# and it publishes every RELEASE at a permanent versioned URL under
# /packages/ with a `.sha256` beside it, which looked like a better durability
# story than the month-end-tag convention this pin used to ride on BtbN (whose
# daily tags are pruned). No signature is offered by either builder; a
# self-computed pinned hash that matches the published one was the honest best
# available, same as the macOS pin above. OUR OWN build is verified one level
# further up, by the FFmpeg project's signature on the source.
#
# WHY SHARED rather than static: unchanged from the BtbN era, and our build
# keeps it. A static build compiles every codec library INTO each executable,
# so ffmpeg.exe, ffprobe.exe and ffplay.exe each carry their own copy of the
# same code; the shared build makes them small stubs beside one set of DLLs.
# The trade is one-sided because the Windows build is --no-default-features: it
# only ever SPAWNS ffmpeg.exe and never links libav, so this is purely a
# packaging-size decision, and the DLLs land exe-adjacent, where
# `util::locate_tool` looks.
#
# The archive's bin/ holds the three exes plus avcodec-63, avdevice-63,
# avfilter-12, avformat-63, avutil-61, swresample-7 and swscale-10 (the
# ffmpeg 9 generation, matching every other platform's bundle). Ship the
# WHOLE of bin/: cherry-picking the exes would produce three binaries that
# cannot start. It is a `.7z`, which costs nothing: the fetch script already
# hard-requires 7-Zip to unpack the tesseract installer.
#
# THE URL BELOW USED TO BE A MIRROR LIST, and it is now a single URL, which is
# the durability problem ENDING rather than a protection being dropped
# (DRAGON-666, DRAGON-675). The claim that gyan.dev "publishes every RELEASE at
# a permanent versioned URL" was FALSE and it cost a release-blocking outage:
# gyan keeps only the CURRENT release build and deletes the previous one the
# moment it publishes the next. Within a day of 9.0.1 appearing, the pinned 9.0
# URL was a hard 404 and every Windows build died in `fetch-win-vendor.ps1`,
# including builds that had nothing to do with ffmpeg. The fix then was to add a
# second URL, our own copy on the `vendor-mirror` release. The fix now is that
# our copy is the ONLY copy: no third party can withdraw it, so there is no
# upstream left to fall back from. `Get-Verified` still accepts a list, so a
# second URL can be added the day one is worth having.
#
# THE 404 IS ALSO WHERE DRAGON-671 CAME FROM, which is the part worth carrying
# forward. The outage forced the pin from 9.0 to 9.0.1 the same day; 9.0.1 had
# been compiled against nv-codec-headers 13.1 where 9.0 used 13.0; the minimum
# NVIDIA driver went to 610.00; NVENC stopped opening on every machine below it
# and recordings fell back to libx265, which cannot encode in real time. So one
# builder's retention policy reached all the way to a customer's broken
# recording, through two pin edits that each looked routine. Owning the build
# removes the whole chain, not just the number at the end of it.
FFMPEG_WINDOWS_X64_URL=https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-9.0-nvcodec-n13.0.19.1-cck1-win64-shared.7z
FFMPEG_WINDOWS_X64_SHA256=13726f06b71368e47fcec9654f30e9d6e1ae75029679ae421b22267048f519aa

# ── The from-source ffmpeg builds' own third-party parts ──────────────────────
#
# Originally the Linux AppImage's alone (DRAGON-510); the Windows cross-build
# (DRAGON-675) reads X264_URL and NVCODEC_URL from here too, which is the whole
# point of it: both platforms' NVENC is compiled against the SAME
# nv-codec-headers pin, so both demand the same NVIDIA driver.
#
# The AppImage bundles MORE third-party code than any other artifact, so it has
# more supply-chain exposure, not less. Everything below is downloaded by
# `scripts/appimage/Dockerfile` while it builds the base image and is verified
# before use.
#
# The AppImage's ffmpeg is built from the SAME `FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL` tarball above,
# with the same GPG check, so the bundled encoder is the pinned 8.1.2 and not a
# second ffmpeg from somewhere else. It is configured far wider than the headers
# build (x264, pulse, VAAPI, NVENC) because the AppImage ships the ffmpeg BINARY
# and its libav libraries, where the zip only needs headers.

# The AppImage runtime: the small static ELF that is concatenated in front of the
# squashfs payload and self-mounts it. Pinned to the DATED release tag, never
# `continuous`: `continuous` is a moving pointer whose asset is replaced in place,
# so it can never carry a checksum that stays true.
#
# STATIC-FUSE, which is the whole reason for taking this rather than a distro
# runtime: Debian 12 and Ubuntu 24.04 dropped libfuse2, and the usual fallback
# (`--appimage-extract-and-run`) unpacks the entire ~100 MB payload on EVERY
# launch. For a one-shot tool fired from PrintScreen that would be an extraction
# per screenshot.
#
# The release also publishes `.sig` files and its own `signing-pubkey.asc`. That
# is not a stronger check than the hash below: both the signature and the key
# come from the same release, so anyone who could replace the artifact could
# replace both. sha256 is the honest description of what we verify.
#
# ONE TAG, one runtime per architecture (DRAGON-529). Both are taken from the
# SAME release: the runtime is what mounts the payload and what sets `$APPIMAGE`,
# which the in-place self-update (DRAGON-532) depends on, so letting the two
# architectures drift to different runtime builds would mean debugging an update
# path that behaves differently per arch for no reason anyone wrote down.
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_TAG=20251108
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_URL=https://github.com/AppImage/type2-runtime/releases/download/20251108/runtime-x86_64
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_SHA256=2fca8b443c92510f1483a883f60061ad09b46b978b2631c807cd873a47ec260d
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_URL_AARCH64=https://github.com/AppImage/type2-runtime/releases/download/20251108/runtime-aarch64
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_SHA256_AARCH64=00cbdfcf917cc6c0ff6d3347d59e0ca1f7f45a6df1a428a0d6d8a78664d87444

# x264, for the bundled ffmpeg's SOFTWARE H.264 encoder (`encode/plan.rs`'s
# software tier is literally `-c:v libx264`). Without it a machine with no VAAPI
# and no NVENC could not record at all, since ffmpeg has no native H.264 encoder.
#
# WHY DEBIAN'S TARBALL rather than videolan's: x264 has no releases and no
# tags. videolan's own snapshot directory stopped in 2019, and code.videolan.org's
# `/-/archive/<commit>` tarballs are GENERATED per request, so their bytes can
# change when GitLab changes its archiver, which would break a pinned hash for a
# reason that is not a supply-chain event. Debian's pool files are immutable by
# policy, and this one is a repack of the upstream git tree at 0480cb0
# (2025-09-10). The hash below is the gate either way.
X264_VERSION=0.165.3223+git20250910.0480cb0
X264_URL=https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/x/x264/x264_0.165.3223+git20250910.0480cb0.orig.tar.xz
X264_SHA256=de68ca43d0c6e09baeb86a38ed5cde86b0a84a21d6babcc569804201dc9df133

# nv-codec-headers: the header-only NVENC/NVDEC interface ffmpeg compiles
# against. It links NOTHING (ffmpeg dlopens `libnvidia-encode` at runtime), so
# this adds no dependency to the shipped binaries; it only decides whether
# `h264_nvenc` / `hevc_nvenc` exist in the bundled ffmpeg and libavcodec at all.
# They must, or every NVIDIA user of the AppImage silently drops to software
# encoding, including on the CUDA zero-copy path (`encode/cuda.rs`).
#
# 13.0.19.x is the series ffmpeg 8.1 requires. GitHub's `/archive/refs/tags/`
# tarballs are generated, like the GitLab ones rejected above; the difference is
# that no immutable alternative exists here, and `TESSERACT_SOURCE_URL` already
# accepts the same risk.
#
# THIS PIN IS THE NVIDIA DRIVER FLOOR, on Linux AND, since DRAGON-675, on
# Windows. The headers carry the NVENC API version ffmpeg compiles against, and
# libnvidia-encode refuses to open when the installed driver implements an older
# one: `Driver does not support the required nvenc API version. Required: 13.1
# Found: 13.0` / `The minimum required Nvidia driver for nvenc is 610.00 or
# newer`. Both numbers are formatted AT RUNTIME from constants compiled in from
# these headers, so nothing in the finished binary can be read to recover them
# (verified with `strings` over a shipped avcodec-63.dll; `-version` and
# `-buildconf` do not report it either). 13.0.19.1 asks for driver ~570.
#
# That unmeasurability is why the Windows ffmpeg is now OUR build. It used to be
# a prebuilt whose header version we inherited, and a routine 9.0 -> 9.0.1 pin
# refresh silently moved the Windows floor to driver 610.00 while Linux stayed
# at ~570, so testers on anything older lost NVENC and fell back to libx265,
# which cannot encode in real time (DRAGON-671). Moving this pin now moves both
# platforms together, visibly, in one line.
NVCODEC_VERSION=n13.0.19.1
NVCODEC_URL=https://github.com/FFmpeg/nv-codec-headers/archive/refs/tags/n13.0.19.1.tar.gz
NVCODEC_SHA256=c293161bd92dac53f2f8fab08abcd4f96773c3644a57b23c2260b4be2311ab2f

# ── The Windows ffmpeg cross-build's own parts (DRAGON-675) ───────────────────
#
# Read ONLY by `scripts/ffmpeg-win/Dockerfile` and `scripts/ffmpeg-win/build.sh`,
# the mingw-w64 container that produces the Windows ffmpeg. Everything the
# Windows build shares with the Linux one (the ffmpeg source tarball, x264,
# nv-codec-headers) is pinned above and deliberately NOT duplicated here.
#
# The four below have no Linux counterpart because the Linux ffmpeg does not
# need them: zlib and SDL2 come from the host there, and AMF and oneVPL are
# Windows-only encoder interfaces.

# zlib, statically linked into the shipped libav DLLs. REQUIRED, not optional:
# ffmpeg's PNG encoder is gated on it, and `preview/video.rs` extracts frames
# with `-f image2pipe -vcodec png`, so a build without zlib produces a preview
# editor that cannot show a video's frames at all.
#
# A GitHub RELEASE ASSET, which is an immutable upload, unlike the generated
# `/archive/` tarballs the AMF and oneVPL pins below have to accept. zlib.net's
# own download directory is not an option: it deletes older versions the moment
# a new one lands, which is the exact gyan.dev failure mode this whole ticket
# exists to remove.
ZLIB_VERSION=1.3.1
ZLIB_URL=https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/v1.3.1/zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz
ZLIB_SHA256=9a93b2b7dfdac77ceba5a558a580e74667dd6fede4585b91eefb60f03b72df23

# x265, the HEVC software encoder tier. `encode/plan.rs`'s software plan uses
# `libx265` whenever the user picks HEVC explicitly, so without it that choice
# fails on every Windows machine with no usable hardware encoder.
#
# TWO URLS, and they are BYTE-IDENTICAL rather than merely equivalent (both
# measured at 40b1ea04… when this pin was made), which is what makes them a
# legal mirror list here: videolan's release mirror and Debian's pool, both
# static uploads rather than generated archives. Bitbucket is upstream's own
# host and is deliberately absent: it answers 403 to a plain download.
X265_VERSION=4.2
X265_URL="https://download.videolan.org/pub/videolan/x265/x265_4.2.tar.gz https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/x/x265/x265_4.2.orig.tar.gz"
X265_SHA256=40b1ea0453e0309f0eba934e0ddf533f8f6295966679e8894e8f1c1c8d5e1210

# SDL2, and it exists for ONE binary: ffplay.exe, which is `ffplay_deps` in
# ffmpeg's own configure. ffplay is the preview editor's audio sink on Windows
# (DRAGON-285), because the bundled ffmpeg has no audio OUTPUT device there the
# way it has `pulse` on Linux and `audiotoolbox` on macOS. Drop this pin and the
# build silently produces no ffplay.exe, `fetch-win-vendor.ps1` dies on the
# missing file, and had it not, video previews would play silently.
#
# Static, so there is no SDL2.dll to ship beside it. A GitHub release asset
# again, so the bytes cannot change under the hash.
SDL2_VERSION=2.32.10
SDL2_URL=https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL/releases/download/release-2.32.10/SDL2-2.32.10.tar.gz
SDL2_SHA256=5f5993c530f084535c65a6879e9b26ad441169b3e25d789d83287040a9ca5165

# AMF, AMD's encoder interface: HEADERS ONLY, like nv-codec-headers. ffmpeg
# dlopens `amfrt64.dll` from the driver at runtime, so this links nothing and
# adds no dependency; it only decides whether `h264_amf` / `hevc_amf` exist at
# all, which is the AMD tier in `encode/plan.rs`'s Windows ladder.
#
# 1.5.2 IS THE FLOOR ffmpeg 9.0 SETS, not a preference: its configure requires
# AMF_VERSION >= 1.5.2.0 and refuses anything older, so this pin cannot move
# down and the tag before it (1.5.0) would fail the build.
#
# The tarball is ~400 MB because the tag archives the whole SDK, samples
# included. Only `amf/public/include` is extracted and the archive is deleted in
# the same docker layer, so the image does not carry it; the cost is one long
# download per pin change, and docker's layer cache pays it once.
AMF_VERSION=1.5.2
AMF_URL=https://github.com/GPUOpen-LibrariesAndSDKs/AMF/archive/refs/tags/v1.5.2.tar.gz
AMF_SHA256=8a70b6dc85261e6e6e57769bd81ac1e09c0a4c96bbd5e358ffbc2dee51e8e50a

# Intel oneVPL, the dispatcher `h264_qsv` / `hevc_qsv` need. ffmpeg 9 offers two
# routes to Quick Sync and refuses both at once: `--enable-libmfx` (the legacy
# Media SDK dispatcher, which ffmpeg's own configure warns is deprecated) and
# `--enable-libvpl`. oneVPL is the one that finds a runtime on current Intel
# hardware, where Media SDK ships no dispatcher at all, so the legacy route
# would quietly cost QSV to exactly the newest Intel machines.
#
# ffmpeg looks this up through pkg-config as `vpl >= 2.6`.
LIBVPL_VERSION=2.17.0
LIBVPL_URL=https://github.com/intel/libvpl/archive/refs/tags/v2.17.0.tar.gz
LIBVPL_SHA256=4de3e2faf1e8307fb282e4a43f443191810f6a6b0a484fffa7995ba1c814c6ec

# OUR OWN build revision, and the one pin here that is not a third-party
# version. It exists because two different archives can carry the same ffmpeg
# version: bump it whenever the configure line, the toolchain or one of the
# pins above changes without FFMPEG_VERSION moving, so the artifact name, the
# `.pinned` stamp in `vendor\` and the mirror asset all change with it.
FFMPEG_WINDOWS_BUILD_REV=1

# ── tesseract (OCR) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Same version on both platforms so OCR behaves identically. macOS builds from
# source because no self-contained arm64 macOS tesseract exists to download;
# Windows takes the project's own official installer, published as a GitHub
# release asset. Neither carries a signature.
TESSERACT_VERSION=5.5.3
TESSERACT_SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract/archive/refs/tags/5.5.3.tar.gz
TESSERACT_SOURCE_SHA256=9218e62793116d42a9f6d14cd9348518b27f382096eea3d0f2d1a24616bb5884
TESSERACT_WINDOWS_INSTALLER_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract/releases/download/5.5.3/tesseract-ocr-w64-setup-5.5.3.20260724.exe
TESSERACT_WINDOWS_INSTALLER_SHA256=bee9e3434bd94fd65387d9be28cd467a41f61b1275383b55b0f59a1331270ae4

# tesseract's dependencies, built statically into the macOS binary so the result
# links nothing outside the bundle. leptonica is built WITHOUT TIFF on purpose;
# scripts/fetch-mac-vendor.sh records why, including the upstream CMake bug that
# makes the alternative impossible.
LEPTONICA_VERSION=1.87.0
LEPTONICA_URL=https://github.com/DanBloomberg/leptonica/releases/download/1.87.0/leptonica-1.87.0.tar.gz
LEPTONICA_SHA256=c73363397f96eb1295602bf44d708a994ad42046c791bf03ea0505d829bdb6a7
LIBPNG_VERSION=1.6.50
# SEVERAL sources, tried in order until one yields the pinned bytes. SourceForge's
# `downloads.sourceforge.net` redirects to a geographically chosen mirror, and a
# dead mirror STALLS AT 0 B/s rather than erroring, which no amount of retrying
# fixes; it hung a mac build twice. The direct `dl.sourceforge.net` hosts skip
# that selection. Listing more than one is safe because the SHA-256 below is the
# gate: a host serving anything else fails as loudly as a single-URL mismatch.
LIBPNG_URL="https://deac-riga.dl.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz https://cytranet-dal.dl.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz https://downloads.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz"
LIBPNG_SHA256=708f4398f996325819936d447f982e0db90b6b8212b7507e7672ea232210949a

# The language pack, the SAME file and hash on both platforms so OCR results
# match. `tessdata_fast` is the LSTM-only, speed-tuned set, which is what a
# screenshot tool wants: `tessdata_best` is several times slower for accuracy
# that does not show up on UI text. Pinned to a tag, not `main`, so the file
# cannot change under the hash.
TESSDATA_TAG=4.1.0
TESSDATA_ENG_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_fast/raw/4.1.0/eng.traineddata
TESSDATA_ENG_SHA256=7d4322bd2a7749724879683fc3912cb542f19906c83bcc1a52132556427170b2

# ── proton-drive CLI (Proton Drive provider, DRAGON-566) ──────────────────────
#
# Proton's own official Drive command-line tool, which the Proton cloud
# provider drives as a child process (src/cloud/proton.rs). Only the FLATPAK
# bundles it: a sandboxed app cannot see a host install at all, so "put it on
# PATH" is advice that package makes false, and the MIT license pinned below is
# what makes redistributing a copy clean. The direct builds keep treating the
# tool as optional and external.
#
# The OFFICIAL prebuilt linux x86_64 binary, from Proton's own download host,
# which keeps every release at a permanent per-version URL. Proton publishes a
# SHA-512 beside each build; the sha256 below was computed from a download
# whose SHA-512 matched Proton's published value, so this pin is anchored to
# what Proton says it shipped. No signature is offered. A linux-arm64 build
# exists at the sibling path (linux-arm64/proton-drive); only x86_64 is pinned
# because the lab Flatpak is x86_64-only.
#
# Flathub follow-up: a submission-clean manifest would build the CLI from the
# ProtonDriveApps/sdk source (it is a Bun project) rather than pin the prebuilt
# binary. Prebuilt here keeps the lab loop short and tests the same bytes
# Proton ships.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE RE-PINNING, and NEVER STRIP: run the downloaded artifact's own
# `./proton-drive version` and require it to print
# `Proton Drive CLI cli-drive@<version>` with exit 0, where <version> matches
# PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_VERSION; a hash alone does not prove the file is the tool it
# claims to be. The binary is a Bun single-file executable (the bun runtime
# with the CLI's JS bundle appended to the ELF), so any ELF rewrite (eu-strip,
# objcopy, flatpak-builder's default debuginfo pass) severs the bundle and
# leaves a bare Bun runtime that self-reports BUN's version (1.3.14 at this
# pin) instead of the CLI's. That is how the fifth live test shipped a tool
# probing Broken while the pin itself was correct; the Flatpak module now sets
# no-debuginfo and the app module re-checks this sha after post-processing.
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_VERSION=0.7.0
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_URL=https://proton.me/download/drive/cli/0.7.0/linux-x64/proton-drive
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_SHA256=4e3c74a7a25da00d7a0ca9ee223a9b7b0b238da357ab6fcfee04a5c313ddf4d1

# The tool's MIT license text, installed beside the bundled binary
# (/app/share/doc/proton-drive/LICENSE.md). Pinned at a COMMIT rather than a
# branch, so the bytes cannot change under the hash.
PROTON_DRIVE_LICENSE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ProtonDriveApps/sdk/941aafa55fb179c8ee347c9d0b4660a3fc893f09/LICENSE.md
PROTON_DRIVE_LICENSE_SHA256=0ce1c7dc272b2b5f86de58045d87dbfdec582fdce352a4a41cc8c56bff51c6f5
